  'node.ratio': 'Traffic ratio', 'node.ratioHelp': 'Traffic through this server counts toward user usage multiplied by this ratio (2 = double); node names get an x2 tag; 1 = as-is',
  'line.ipVersion': 'IP version', 'line.ipVersionHelp': 'This server is dual-stack, so this line can use IPv4 or IPv6 on its own; by default it follows the Servers page.', 'line.famDefault': 'Default ({f})', 'line.famDefaultPlain': 'Default', 'line.famDefaultDns': 'Default (DNS)', 'line.famPick': 'This server is dual-stack, so this line can use IPv4 or IPv6 on its own.', 'line.famPickMulti': 'After ticking a dual-stack server you can choose next to it whether this line uses IPv4 or IPv6 there.',
  'line.famDnsHelp': 'Subscriptions give domains as node addresses: lines that pick nothing give the domain and DNS on the client decides IPv4 or IPv6, so "Auto address uses" on the server has no effect; lines that pick a version still give that IP directly.',
  'line.v6Tips': 'IPv6 checklist (this line uses IPv6 on some servers)', 'line.v6Status': 'IPv6 inbound check (Servers page, in the same round as mainland reach):', 'line.v6Never': 'not checked yet',
  'line.v6Tip1': 'The IPv6 address must accept inbound connections: WARP, NAT66 and egress-only gateways show an egress address that cannot be reached; trust the IPv6 inbound check above (a 1:1 NATed public IPv6, as on Azure, is not on the NIC and still works).',
  'line.v6Tip2': 'Open the port for ::/0 separately in the cloud security group (hysteria2 / tuic use UDP, the rest TCP); the subnet needs an IPv6 range and a ::/0 route to an internet gateway (not an egress-only one); Alibaba Cloud also needs IPv6 public bandwidth enabled.',
  'line.v6Tip3': 'Allow IPv6 in the server firewall too: check ip6tables -S, nft list ruleset, ufw status verbose; flushing iptables does not open IPv6.',
  'line.v6Tip4': 'Capture packets: run tcpdump -ni any ip6 and port <port> on the server (hysteria2 / tuic use UDP: ip6 and udp port <port>; for a hysteria2 line with port hopping capture the range too: tcpdump -ni any "ip6 and (udp port <port> or udp portrange <from>-<to>)", since clients send to ports in that range and tcpdump sees them before they are redirected to the line port) and connect once from a client. tcpdump sees packets before the server firewall, so look for replies: no packets at all means the cloud security group, routing or the client network; packets coming in but no replies (TCP: SYN retransmitted, no SYN-ACK; UDP: inbound only): on a hysteria2 line with obfuscation (obfs) check the client\'s obfuscation password first, because with a wrong password or obfuscation off on the client the server drops the packets silently, which looks just like a firewall drop; once that is ruled out (or without obfs), the server firewall (ip6tables / nftables / ufw) drops them, or replies leave through the wrong route; traffic both ways but still no connection means a certificate, SNI, password or device-limit problem (see Recent inbound connections on the dashboard; hysteria2 / tuic certificate and SNI errors are not recorded there).',
  'line.v6Tip5': 'The client network needs IPv6 (curl -6 https://www.cloudflare.com/cdn-cgi/trace works) and the IPv6 switch in the client must be on.',
  'line.v6Tip6': 'With a device limit, IPv4 addresses and IPv6 /64s are counted separately, so a dual-stack device does not count twice.',
  'line.famHelp': 'After ticking a dual-stack server you can choose next to it whether this line uses IPv4 or IPv6 there (default: the server\'s own setting).', 'line.servers': 'Deploy on servers', 'line.allServers': 'all',
  'set.subServerAddr': 'Node address in subscriptions', 'set.subServerAddrHelp': 'Default: each server\'s public IP (domain only as SNI), so poisoned local DNS cannot break connections; choose "domain" to use hostnames', 'set.addrIp': 'IP (recommended)', 'set.addrDomain': 'domain',
  'dash.recentConns': 'Recent inbound connections', 'dash.recentConnsHelp': 'Inbound connections that passed the protocol handshake and authentication. Connections refused by a device limit or a reseller device pool are listed too, in red. A wrong certificate, SNI, REALITY setting, password or UUID shows up under the "Handshake failed" filter, but these are never recorded: hysteria2 / tuic QUIC handshake failures (untrusted certificate, wrong SNI / ALPN) and a wrong hysteria2 obfuscation password; connections to REALITY lines that close at once or send something other than TLS (port scans, reachability probes); TLS failures inside ws / grpc transports; shadowsocks, socks, http and mixed lines; older nodes that do not report this. So your IP missing from both does not prove packets never reached the server. Capturing packets on the server is the most reliable check (tcpdump -ni any port <port>, udp port for hysteria2 / tuic, plus udp portrange <from>-<to> for the hopping range when port hopping is on); for TCP lines you can also set the log level to info on the Logs page and look for "inbound connection from" (hysteria2 / tuic only log it after authentication).',
  'dash.failsTitle': 'Arrived but failed the handshake / authentication', 'dash.failWhy': 'Reason', 'dash.failsOldNodes': 'These servers run an older version and do not report handshake / authentication failures, so certificate or password problems on them never show up under "Handshake failed" and their absence proves nothing: {names}.',
  'dash.failsHelp': 'These sources reached the server but failed the TLS / REALITY handshake (certificate, SNI, REALITY public key / short_id) or protocol authentication (password, UUID), so they were not let in. If your own IP is here, the network path works and the client config needs fixing; an auth failure can also mean the user is disabled, expired, over quota or not assigned this line (its credentials were withdrawn from the server), so check the user first. Unknown IPs are usually scanners. REALITY lines only record clients that sent a TLS handshake. Kept and limited separately, so they cannot push out the Connections list; when there are many, each filter keeps each server\'s latest entry and fill the rest by time, so a server flooded by scanners cannot push out another server\'s latest entry.',
  'dash.fail.tls': 'TLS handshake failed', 'dash.fail.tls.help': 'The TLS / REALITY handshake failed: the certificate is not trusted, the SNI is wrong, the REALITY public key or short_id is wrong, or the client did not use TLS. A reason saying failed to dial dest means the REALITY handshake target is unreachable, so nobody can connect: change the line\'s handshake target.',
  'dash.fail.auth': 'Auth failed', 'dash.fail.auth.help': 'The handshake succeeded but protocol authentication failed: wrong password / UUID, or not a client of this protocol; the user may also be disabled, expired, over quota or not assigned this line (credentials withdrawn). hysteria2 and tuic only record this kind; their handshake failures are not recorded.', 'dash.noConns': 'No inbound connections yet', 'dash.rejectedDevice': 'Refused: device limit', 'dash.rejectedPool': 'Refused: reseller device pool full', 'dash.rejectedHelp': 'This connection authenticated, but the user already has the maximum number of devices online (IPv4 addresses and IPv6 /64s are counted separately), so it was refused. It gets in once another device has been idle for a minute, or raise the device limit.', 'dash.connCount': 'Count', 'dash.connLast': 'Last',
  'mig.s4': 'Retire the old server', 'mig.s4h': 'Once users are online on the new server, run systemctl disable --now m-ui on the old one (stops it and keeps it from starting at boot), or uninstall it with "卸载 m-ui" in the m-ui menu. Stopping alone is not enough: after a reboot the old server starts again and keeps serving and pushing stale data. Replacing a secondary server is simpler: install it, then update its API URL and token on the Servers page; the hub re-pushes automatically.',
  'mig.cmdHelp': 'Replace the path at the end with the backup you copied to the new server (named like m-ui-20261111-030000.zip). The same command works on amd64 and arm64; the script installs the latest version first, then restores.',
